<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Supplies – Lesson # 2</h2>
<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Polite Request</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Can I Have a Pencil?</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
  </tr>
  <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of texts by answering both written and oral questions.<br>Reading: Identify key details in a story.<br>Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show understanding of polite request texts by writing simple responses (e.g., "Can I have a pencil?" "Yes, here.").<br>Can identify details in a story to answer questions (e.g., "Can she help her friend?" "No, she cannot.").<br>Can identify and blend individual phonemes to create one-syllable or two-syllable familiar words (e.g., /d-ɛ-s-k/ in the word "desk").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Stage & Description</h3></th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</h3></th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Estimated Date and Time</h3></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge, and introduce the theme and language through modeling and clarification.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of key vocabulary from Lesson 1: "pencil," "paper," "desk," "friend," "bag." Students identify the words aloud. The teacher then writes the words on the board. For phonemic awareness, the teacher slowly pronounces a word, e.g., /p-ɛ-n-s-ɪ-l/, and asks students to blend the sounds to say the whole word "pencil" as they read it on the board. Repeat for "desk" /d-ɛ-s-k/ and "kind" /k-aɪ-n-d/.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading a simple request on the board: "Can I have a pencil, please?" The teacher points to each word as they read, emphasizing "please" and the /p/ sound in "please." The teacher then models reading a simple answer: "Yes, here you are. Thank you."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of politeness.
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we say 'please' when we ask for something?" (Yes/No with thumbs up/down).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it polite to say 'Thank you'?" (Yes/No).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In this stage, students are exposed to new language content within an authentic text, focusing on the scenario "A Polite Request."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher distributes a short, illustrated story or dialogue titled "The Missing Pencil." The story features two friends, Leo and Mia, interacting at school, using polite requests and responses. The text incorporates grammar (Can I have...please? I can't. I have/don't have), vocabulary (pencil, paper, friend, ask, help, desk, kind, please, thank you), and pragmatic functions (making polite requests, expressing inability politely).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt, 3-4 sentences long, printed on a worksheet with simple illustrations. The register is informal and friendly, suitable for young learners.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understands and interprets simple written texts related to everyday situations and school environment.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the story silently first. Then, the teacher reads it aloud while students follow along.
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question (oral):</b> "What does Leo need?" (Students answer orally, e.g., "A pencil.").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (written):</b> Students read two simple statements and circle 'True' or 'False' based on the text. For example: "Leo has a pencil. True / False." "Mia helps Leo. True / False."</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on developing accuracy in reading, using the target language and content introduced in the presentation stage.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension of polite requests and identifying key details. Grammar: Polite requests ("Can I have...please?"), Negative contracted form of can ("I can't"), Present simple ("I have a pencil, but I don't have an eraser."). Vocabulary: pencil, paper, friend, help, ask, please, thank you.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with the story "The Missing Pencil" again, but with 3-4 key words missing (e.g., "pencil," "please," "help," "can't"). Students choose the correct words from a word bank at the top of the worksheet to fill in the blanks, ensuring they read the context carefully.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Reading Activity (Sentence Matching):</b> The teacher gives students a worksheet with sentences from the story on one side (e.g., "Leo needs a ____.") and pictures representing "pencil," "paper," "desk" on the other. Students draw lines to match the sentence to the correct picture. This reinforces reading for details and word recognition.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness & Word Recognition:</b> The teacher writes a few words from the story on the board, e.g., "desk," "hand," "kind." The teacher points to "desk" and asks students to blend the sounds /d-ɛ-s-k/ to say "desk." Students repeat. Repeat for "hand" /h-æ-n-d/ and "kind" /k-aɪ-n-d/. This connects sounds to written words.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language in a realistic communicative task, applying their reading comprehension skills in the context of "A Polite Request."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students read a short, new scenario and write a simple polite request or answer.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual written response based on a guided reading prompt.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher gives each student a worksheet with two new, short scenarios (1-2 sentences each) and a blank line for a response.
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Scenario 1:</b> "Your friend, Ana, needs paper for drawing. What does she say to you?" (Students write: "Can I have paper, please?").</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Scenario 2:</b> "Your friend, Tom, asks you for an eraser: 'Can I have an eraser, please?' You have one. What do you say?" (Students write: "Yes, here you are. Thank you.").</li>
          </ul>
        The teacher walks around, monitors, and provides support as students read and write their answers. This reinforces reading comprehension for specific information and simple written production of polite requests/responses.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying key information, and writing simple, grammatically correct polite requests/responses using target vocabulary.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage involves formative assessment to check learning and allows students to reflect on their performance.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the performance stage and quick check of written responses.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete an exit ticket. They read a very short dialogue (2 sentences) on the exit ticket and answer one written comprehension question.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue:</b><br>Sarah: "Can I have a pencil, please?"<br>Mark: "I can't help you right now. I don't have a pencil."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Question:</b> "Does Mark have a pencil?" (Students write: "No, he doesn't." or "No.").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of the written response to the comprehension question.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identification of key details in the text (e.g., who has what, who can help).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of "can't" if applicable.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer two questions on their exit ticket:
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read a story about a polite request." (Circle: Yes / No).</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write a simple answer to a question about the story." (Circle: Yes / No).</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages both student and teacher to reflect on the learning process and connect to future lessons.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for you today when reading the story?"</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was a little difficult for you today?"</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
          <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 5px;">
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students successfully identified key details in the simple story and were able to answer basic comprehension questions. The phoneme blending activity helped reinforce word recognition for "desk" and "kind."</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students still confuse "have" and "don't have" in their written responses, requiring more explicit practice. I need to continue reinforcing the pronunciation of /p/ in "please" and /k/ in "can't" during oral reading.</li>
            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For the next lesson, I will ensure students have opportunities to orally practice the polite requests they read today.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Today we practiced reading stories and requests. Next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), we will practice *speaking* and *acting out* these polite requests with our friends to borrow school supplies."</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>